    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tuoba"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tuoba.jpg" alt="Tuoba" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tuoba">Tuoba</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Northern_Wei_soldier.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gary Todd, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The origins of the Rouran are known almost entirely from Chinese histories, above all the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-wei">Wei shu</a></em> of the Northern Wei, their enemies. According to this account their ancestor Mugulü was a slave seized by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Tuoba Xianbei</a> raiders in the late 3rd century, who served as a cavalryman, fled to escape punishment and gathered a band of fugitives in the steppe north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobi-desert">Gobi</a>. His son Cheluhui organised them as a tribe, which took the name Rouran and paid tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuoba">Tuoba</a> state of Dai. The name appears in Chinese in several forms, and the Northern Wei emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taiwu-of-northern-wei">Taiwu</a> ordered it written Ruanruan, 'wriggling insects', as an insult. The Rouran were a mixed confederation whose ruling clan, the Yujiulü, probably spoke a Mongolic or related language, though this remains uncertain. When the Northern Wei, founded by the Tuoba in 386, campaigned against them, the chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yujiulu-shelun">Shelun</a> led his people north into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, conquered the Gaoche (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiele">Tiele</a>) tribes and other groups, and in 402 took the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a> with the regnal name Qiudoufa. Shelun is the first ruler recorded as using khagan as the title of a supreme steppe sovereign, although the word itself may already have been current among the Xianbei, and under him the Rouran became a true steppe empire.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/turfan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/turfan.jpg" alt="Turfan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turfan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turpan-jiaohe-ruinas-d16.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Colegota, CC BY-SA 2.5 es</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Rouran lived as pastoral nomads, moving with herds of horses, sheep, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a> between summer pastures in the north and winter grounds near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobi-desert">Gobi</a>, and living in felt tents. The <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-wei">Wei shu</a></em> states that they had no writing, at first counting with sheep droppings and later keeping records by notching wood. Their wealth came largely from tribute, raiding and control of other peoples. Subject groups such as the Gaoche and the Turks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altai-mountains">Altai</a>, who worked iron for them, supplied livestock, warriors and metal, and the oasis states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turfan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> paid tribute and at times accepted Rouran-backed rulers. The Rouran traded horses and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> on the Chinese frontier and sent embassies to the Southern Dynasties, whose courts they reached by roundabout routes through the Tuyuhun lands or the western oases, and they took part in exchange along the steppe and oasis routes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>. In the early 6th century, especially under Anagui, the court grew more elaborate, with Chinese advisers and marriage alliances with the Northern Wei and its successor states. Religion combined steppe cults of Heaven with shamanic practices, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> also reached the court: in 511 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yujiulu-chounu">Chounu</a> sent a monk as his envoy to the Northern Wei.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gaochang"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gaochang.jpg" alt="Gaochang" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gaochang">Gaochang</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turpan-gaochang-d10.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Colegota, CC BY-SA 2.5 es</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Like other steppe powers, the Rouran fought as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mounted-archer">mounted archers</a> organised in decimal units; the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-wei">Wei shu</a></em> reports that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yujiulu-shelun">Shelun</a> set up a military organisation in units of a thousand and of a hundred men, each under its own commander, with laws rewarding bravery and punishing cowardice. Their tactics relied on raiding, rapid movement and withdrawal into the steppe when faced with superior forces. For a century and a half the Rouran were the chief enemy of the Northern Wei. In 424 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yujiulu-datan">Datan</a> raided the old Wei centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shengle">Shengle</a> with a large cavalry army, but Wei armies campaigned deep into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> repeatedly, and Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taiwu-of-northern-wei">Taiwu</a>'s great expedition of 429 surprised the Rouran, forcing Datan to flee and capturing large numbers of people and animals. The Northern Wei fortified its frontier with the Six Garrisons and long walls. The Rouran in turn sought alliances with the southern Chinese courts and with states such as the Northern Liang, and fought the Gaoche, who broke away in 487 under Afuzhiluo and founded an independent kingdom in the west; in 508 the Gaoche king Mi'etu killed the khagan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yujiulu-futu">Futu</a> in battle. Rouran armies also pushed into the oases, and in 460 they took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaochang">Gaochang</a> from the last Northern Liang ruler and installed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kan-bozhou">Kan Bozhou</a> as king there.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/song-dynasty.jpg" alt="Song Dynasty" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:China_-_Song_Dynasty-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">China - Song Dynasty - cs.svg: User:Mozzan derivative work: Kanguole, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Rouran state was a confederation held together by the Yujiulü royal clan. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a> ruled from a mobile court, and Chinese sources mention a royal seat called Mumo, whose location is uncertain. Each khagan took a regnal title in the Rouran language, transcribed in Chinese, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yujiulu-shelun">Shelun</a>'s Qiudoufa, which the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-wei">Wei shu</a></em> glosses as 'controlling and expanding'. Succession was contested among brothers, uncles and sons within the ruling clan, and several khagans were overthrown or killed by relatives. Beneath the khagan, members of the royal clan and the chiefs of allied tribes commanded their own followings, while subject peoples such as the Gaoche and the Turks were governed through their own leaders and obliged to send tribute and warriors. The khagan's chief consort bore the title <em>khatun</em>, recorded in Chinese as <em>kehedun</em>, and both titles passed to later steppe states. After Anagui's restoration with Northern Wei help in the early 520s, his court adopted Chinese-style official titles and employed Chinese advisers, according to the Chinese histories. The Rouran also conducted wide diplomacy, exchanging embassies with the Northern Wei, the Liu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, the Southern Qi and Liang, the Tuyuhun and western powers, and using marriage alliances with the Eastern and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-wei">Western Wei</a> to play the divided north Chinese states against each other.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate.jpg" alt="Khazar Khaganate" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chasaren.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Rouran Khaganate's most lasting legacy is the steppe political tradition it handed on. The titles <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a>, for the supreme ruler, and khatun, for his consort, became established at the Rouran court and were taken over by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-turkic-khaganate">Turkic Khaganate</a>, which arose among the Rouran's own subjects, and later by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>. The Turks also inherited the Rouran pattern of a royal clan ruling a confederation of tribes from the Mongolian steppe and extracting tribute from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and the oasis cities. The Rouran also shaped the history of northern China: the long struggle against them encouraged the Northern Wei to keep a heavily militarised frontier, and the Six Garrisons built to contain them became the centre of the great revolt of the 520s that broke the Northern Wei apart. In the west Rouran influence reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turfan</a>, the Tarim oases and possibly the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephthalites">Hephthalites</a>. The fate of the Rouran after 555 has prompted a long debate. Byzantine writers record that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> who arrived on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> steppe around 557 were fleeing the Turks, and some historians identify them, or their ruling group, with Rouran refugees; others reject the link. Recent ancient DNA studies of early Avar elite burials in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> found ancestry closely matching populations of the Mongolian steppe, which some researchers see as supporting a Rouran connection.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/changan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/changan.jpg" alt="Chang'an" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prince_Yide%27s_tomb,_towers.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown Tang-era tomb artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Rouran Khaganate declined through internal division and the revolt of its subjects. The defection of the Gaoche in 487 deprived it of many tribes, and dynastic murders weakened the royal clan; in 520 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yujiulu-chounu">Chounu</a> was killed, and his brother Anagui, driven out by a rival, fled to the Northern Wei, which restored him with its support. Anagui rebuilt Rouran power in the 520s and 530s, profiting from the collapse of the Northern Wei into the rival Eastern and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-wei">Western Wei</a>, from both of which he received gifts and marriage alliances, and by about 541 he had crushed the Gaoche. But the Turks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altai-mountains">Altai</a>, once ironworkers for the Rouran, grew strong under their leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bumin-qaghan">Bumin</a>, who in 546 defeated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiele">Tiele</a> force marching against the Rouran and then asked Anagui for a royal bride. Anagui refused, insulting him as a blacksmith slave, and Bumin turned to the Western Wei, which gave him a princess in 551. In 552 Bumin defeated the Rouran north of Huaihuang and Anagui killed himself. Rouran remnants under rival leaders fought on, some taking refuge with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-qi">Northern Qi</a> or the Western Wei, until the Turkic khagan Muqan crushed them; in 555 the Western Wei handed about 3,000 Rouran refugees to Turkic envoys, who killed them outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Rouran Khaganate ruled the Mongolian steppe for a century and a half between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> and the Turks, was the chief northern enemy of the Northern Wei, and established <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a> as the title of supreme steppe rule, a tradition inherited by the Turks and later steppe empires.</em></p>

